Summary
Implemented canonical Workload Manifest v1 with strict resource requirements, expected-output and integrity hashes, bounded retry policies, deterministic JSON serialization, Ed25519-compatible signing, fail-closed verification, and strict wire-format parsing. 

Added the deterministic distributed scheduler with workload priorities, logical-tick starvation prevention, retry backoff, CPU capacity accounting, memory and CPU/GPU/NPU capability matching, trust-aware placement, and stable node-ID tie breaking. 

Implemented the authenticated-node execution lifecycle covering assignment, acknowledgement, execution, monotonic progress, completion, checksum verification, corruption rejection, deterministic duplicate sampling, failure recovery, and trust-score updates. 

Added reproducible mixed CPU/GPU/NPU simulations for 10, 100, 500, and 1000 nodes, measuring throughput, assignment latency, peak utilization, verification success, and Jain’s fairness index. 

Restored the legacy ZDXNode public API alongside ZDXNodeAgent and corrected the authentication integration-test callback contract. 

Updated the architecture, roadmap, changelog, TODO, security model, test plan, development status, and node operator documentation. Pass 3 is explicitly complete for the repository scope, while Pass 4 remains unstarted. 

Documented audit findings and production boundaries: durable replicated scheduling, execution sandboxing, lease/crash recovery, Byzantine verification, accelerator accounting, observability, and removal of tracked bytecode remain future work. 

Documented monetization opportunities including verified-compute APIs, reserved capacity, enterprise private fabrics, accelerator marketplace pricing, verification/provenance packages, managed node operations, and fleet analytics. 

Created six logical commits on the current branch and created the pull request titled “Complete Pass 3 deterministic distributed scheduling.”

